  "answer": "Mendeleev’s periodic table had several limitations. One major limitation was the presence of anomalous pairs of elements, where elements with higher atomic weights were placed before those with lower atomic weights. For example, tellurium (Te) with an atomic weight of 127.6 was placed before iodine (I) with an atomic weight of 126.9. Mendeleev accepted these minor inversions because they resulted in elements being placed in the correct groups. Another limitation was the placement of dissimilar elements in the same group, such as alkali metals (like Li, Na, K) being grouped with coinage metals (like Cu, Ag, Au), which have different properties. Additionally, the table could not accurately predict the properties of undiscovered elements, although Mendeleev successfully predicted the properties of elements like gallium, germanium, and scandium. The modern periodic table addresses these limitations by arranging elements based on their atomic numbers rather than atomic weights. This change eliminates the problem of anomalous series and ensures that elements with similar properties are grouped together. The modern periodic table also provides a more accurate representation of the electronic configurations of elements, which explains their chemical and physical properties more effectively.",
